# they-do-not-stack — plan 11 F5c.
#
# Three enemies leave ONE spawn hex and immediately take TWO different
# routes to the core.  `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing the
# perimeter: *"An enemy whose target hex is taken by a companion does
# not wait behind it — it moves beside them."*
#
# ── Why this world and not a corridor ───────────────────────────
#
# Every other enemy scenario here walks a ONE-HEX-WIDE corridor, and
# in a corridor there is no "beside": the field offers exactly one
# closer neighbour, so a blocked enemy can only wait, and F5c looks
# like a queue.  Measured (plan 11 § F5c): on a hex AXIS exactly one
# neighbour is closer; OFF the axis, two are.
#
# So this world is an open field — rows r = 0 to 4, q = 0 to 8 — and
# the spawn sits at (8, 4), off every axis.  The distance to the core
# over it is simply q + r, and (8, 4) has two neighbours at 11:
#
#   (8, 3)  — direction 3, so it wins the tie
#   (7, 4)  — direction 4
#
# Three enemies, two closer hexes: the first takes (8, 3), the second
# CANNOT and takes (7, 4) instead of waiting, and the third has nowhere
# closer to go and holds the spawn for a tick.  That is the whole
# phase, stated as three coordinates.
#
# ⚠ `enemies distinct` is RED the moment the wave spawns — three
# enemies on the marker hex — and green one tick later.  A wave spawns
# stacked; it is walking that spreads it.

echo they do not stack: one spawn hex, two routes, three enemies

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn at (8, 4) — twelve hexes out, so clear of
# close_spawn_disable_radius (10) and well inside the scrambler
# bubble (25), which means the FIELD steers from the first tick and
# the heading never gets a say.
# ⚠ TWICE: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower -> spawn
# since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 10 4
marker 10 4 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

# The open field: five rows of nine.
palette 5
drag 0 0 8 0
drag 0 1 8 1
drag 1 2 9 2
drag 1 3 9 3
drag 2 4 10 4

count painted 45 45
kind 10 4 grass
kind 0 0 grass

wave 3
count alive 3 3
enemy 0 10 4
enemy 1 10 4
enemy 2 10 4
enemies passable
range 12 12
snap spread-wave-starts

# ⚠ **The pre-walk window** (plan 16 W2): 8 ticks standing, all three
# still stacked on the marker — so the spread below is the STEERING and
# not merely time passing.
tick 8
enemy 0 10 4
enemy 1 10 4
enemy 2 10 4
range 12 12

# ⚠ THE PHASE.  One tick, and the three enemies that all wanted (8, 3)
# are on three different hexes: the first took it, the second went
# BESIDE it to (7, 4) — a hex on a different route home — and the
# third had no closer hex left and held the spawn.
tick 1
enemy 0 9 3
enemy 1 9 4
enemy 2 10 4
enemies distinct
enemies passable
range 11 12
snap spread-the-fork

# And they keep the fork: two files walking home a hex apart, with the
# third now moving too.
tick 1
enemy 0 9 2
enemy 1 8 3
enemy 2 9 3
enemies distinct
range 10 11

# Home.  ⚠ `range 0 1` and not `0 0`: the core hex holds ONE enemy and
# the others take hexes BESIDE it.  Before F5c all three would read
# (0, 0) — a wave that arrives is a wave that spreads out, not a wave
# that vanishes into one hex.
#
# ⚠ **Plan 11 F7b moved the third one from (2, 0) to (0, 1)**, and the
# difference is the whole of that phase.  It used to queue two hexes
# back on the approach axis, because the only hex closer than it was
# taken and F5c had it wait there.  It now steps BESIDE — same distance
# from the core, a different face of it — which is what
# `ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing the perimeter always said a blocked
# enemy does.  Three arrivals, three faces of the core, nothing
# queueing.
#
# ⚠ And the one that arrives first is `enemy 1`, not `enemy 0`.  The
# two files rejoin at (7, 0), and the enemy on the (7, 4) route reaches
# the merge one tick before the enemy on the (8, 3) route needs it —
# so the leader out of the spawn yields.  Traffic, and it is
# repeatable: the move order is closest-to-the-core first, ties by hex,
# so the same script gives the same three hexes every run.
tick 20
enemy 0 0 1
enemy 1 0 0
enemy 2 1 0
enemies distinct
enemies passable
range 0 1
count alive 3 3
